Troubleshooting email issues involves a structured approach based on where the problem lies
- sending, receiving, authentication, spam filtering, or client-side configuration. Here's a practical guide you can follow:

✅ 1. Identify the Type of Issue
Ask or observe:
Is the user unable to send, receive, or both?
Is it happening to all users or just one?
Is there any bounce-back message or error code?

📤 2. Troubleshooting Sending Issues (SMTP)
🔍 Check 1: SMTP Settings in Client (e.g., Outlook)
Correct SMTP server (e.g., smtp.example.com)
Port: 587 (STARTTLS) or 465 (SSL)
Correct email and password
Use of SSL/TLS enabled
🔍 Check 2: Firewall or ISP Blocking Port 25
Use telnet smtp.example.com 25 or openssl s_client -connect smtp.example.com:465


commands - mailq , postqueue -p , exim -bp

🔍 Check 5: DNS Records (SPF, DKIM, DMARC)
Use online tools (e.g., mxtoolbox.com) to check
Missing or incorrect records can cause email delivery to spam

📥 3. Troubleshooting Receiving Issues (IMAP/POP)
🔍 Check 1: IMAP/POP Configuration
IMAP port: 993 (SSL) / 143 (STARTTLS)
POP port: 995 (SSL) / 110 (STARTTLS)
🔍 Check 2: Disk Quota or Mailbox Full
Check /var/mail/username or webmail interface

Restart outlook or mailapp

🌍 5. Check Domain/DNS Issues
Use dig, nslookup or online tools to verify:
MX records point to correct mail server

